[stderr] # 03 — Technical, data, trust, and quality contract
[stderr] 
[stderr] **Inputs:** [`INITIAL.md`](../INITIAL.md), [product brief](01-product-brief.md),
[stderr] and [feature model](02-feature-model.md). This contract makes the P0 offline
[stderr] single-player release implementable without selecting a vendor or creating a
[stderr] dependency on the contradictory connected-service requests. It is a design
[stderr] contract, not permission to collect data or operate services.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Architecture and requirement traceability
[stderr] 
[stderr] The production architecture is a static, client-side 2D web game: a shared
[stderr] deterministic domain core, browser presentation/input adapters, versioned
[stderr] authored content, and local durable storage. The web host may deliver a signed
[stderr] release bundle and service worker, but it is not a campaign authority. The
[stderr] core must have no browser APIs, timers, network calls, or renderer dependencies.
[stderr] 
[stderr] | Component / boundary | Requirement served | Contract |
[stderr] | --- | --- | --- |
[stderr] | Game shell and responsive renderer | J1, J3, J6; readable mobile-to-desktop play | Owns navigation, focus/visibility pause, semantic UI, visual settings, and rendering only. It requests domain commands and renders returned state/events. |
[stderr] | Input adapters | Keyboard, mouse, touch, gamepad accessibility | Convert platform events into a shared, remappable `InputAction` vocabulary; unavailable hardware is non-fatal. No adapter may mutate campaign state directly. |
[stderr] | Deterministic rules core | Tutorial combat, quests, inventory, progression | Owns legal state transitions, seeded/randomness policy, combat resolution, reward ledger, and invariants. Command validation precedes every mutation. |
[stderr] | Content registry and validator | One town/dungeon, original content, localisation | Loads the active versioned content package; resolves IDs/keys and rejects invalid packages before play. It is read-only at runtime. |
[stderr] | Save/recovery repository | J5, long-session recovery | Owns atomic local snapshot writes, validation, quarantine, migration, retention, export/import when approved, and explicit deletion. |
[stderr] | PWA release manager | J7, install/offline/update safety | Owns precache manifest, cache integrity, install affordance, and safe-boundary activation. It never replaces a running release or a pending save write. |
[stderr] | Error boundary and consent-gated diagnostics | Crash/error recovery and privacy | Shows retry/title/recovery actions. Local diagnostic records are minimal and removable; no telemetry transport exists in P0. |
[stderr] 
[stderr] ### Dependency direction and state rules
[stderr] 
[stderr] ```text
[stderr] authored content ──> content validator ──> deterministic rules core <── input actions
[stderr]                                             │
[stderr] web/PWA shell + renderer <── state/events ─┼──> save/recovery repository
[stderr]         │                                   │
[stderr]         └──────────> PWA release manager ──┴──> error boundary / local diagnostics
[stderr] ```
[stderr] 
[stderr] Only the rules core can produce durable campaign state. The shell owns
[stderr] ephemeral UI state (open panels, selection, animation), while the repository
[stderr] owns only serialized snapshots and recovery metadata. Rendering, input,
[stderr] caching, and diagnostics must not be required to make a legal game decision.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Data contract
[stderr] 
[stderr] ### Ownership, lifecycle, and retention
[stderr] 
[stderr] | Data class | Owner and lifecycle | Persistence / retention | Access and recovery |
[stderr] | --- | --- | --- | --- |
[stderr] | Content package | Build/content owner: authored → schema-validated → bundled → immutable release artifact → superseded | Public static assets; current and previous known-good cache retained per cache policy | Read by client only; invalid references reject package before gameplay. |
[stderr] | Campaign snapshot | Player; new game → checkpoint/manual save → valid, migrated, quarantined, or explicitly deleted | Browser-origin local storage appropriate for structured durable data (implementation selects IndexedDB or equivalent); retain current valid slot plus last-known-good checkpoint | Same browser origin only in P0; player may load, delete after confirmation, and receive export/support guidance where feasible. |
[stderr] | Settings | Player; default → previewed → applied → persisted/reset | Separate local preference record; current session remains active if persistence fails | Read/write only by local game; reset requires confirmation. |
[stderr] | Recovery metadata | Client; write attempt/failure → recovery offer → resolved | Local, bounded and tied to save slot; remove with slot/delete-local-data action | Never overwrites valid snapshot; aids quarantine and fallback. |
[stderr] | Diagnostic record | Player-controlled, consent-gated; fault → local record → optional future export/delete | Bounded local record, no identifiers, no campaign dialogue, and no network sending in P0 | Player can decline, clear, or export only after a future approved workflow. |
[stderr] 
[stderr] The local campaign is the P0 source of truth. There is no account, remote
[stderr] profile, analytics stream, purchase ledger, match state, or cloud backup.
[stderr] Remote account-linked replication is a later optional mirror—not campaign truth
[stderr] until approval defines identity, vendor, consent, cost, retention, deletion,
[stderr] support, conflict UX, and outage behaviour.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ### Versioned content and save schemas
[stderr] 
[stderr] All high-volume authored data uses a versioned, machine-validated package:
[stderr] 
[stderr] ```text
[stderr] ContentPackage { packageId, semanticVersion, schemaVersion, localeDefault,
[stderr]   scenes[], exits[], dialogues[], quests[], encounters[], enemyDefinitions[],
[stderr]   itemDefinitions[], rewards[], localisation[], assetManifest[] }
[stderr] SaveSnapshot { schemaVersion, contentPackageId, contentVersion, slotId,
[stderr]   revision, checkpointId, campaign, quests, party, inventory, rewardLedger,
[stderr]   settingsReference?, checksum, committedAt }
[stderr] ```
[stderr] 
[stderr] - **Ownership and authoring:** designated game/content owners maintain source
[stderr]   data in repository-controlled files and original asset references. The exact
[stderr]   editor/format is an implementation choice; generated bundles are derived,
[stderr]   never hand-edited.
[stderr] - **Validation:** CI validates JSON/schema shape, unique stable IDs, required
[stderr]   fields, asset and localisation-key existence, valid quest predicates,
[stderr]   scene/spawn/exit targets, legal reward/item/enemy references, no duplicate
[stderr]   reward IDs, and reachability from opening scene to ending. It also rejects
[stderr]   missing required translations in any release locale and unsafe quest-item
[stderr]   removal paths.
[stderr] - **Relationship rules:** every required quest path has a reachable transition;
[stderr]   each locked exit predicate refers to an existing quest state; each encounter
[stderr]   has legal targets/actions and a completion path; all rewards resolve to
[stderr]   extant definitions. Tests include valid minimal package, each broken
[stderr]   reference class, and a graph traversal proving the authored campaign is
[stderr]   completable.
[stderr] - **Import/export:** P0 imports content only at build time through the
[stderr]   validator and packages it into the release. Player save export/import is not
[stderr]   promised; if added, it must use a versioned envelope, parse/validate before
[stderr]   write, never replace a valid save until confirmation, and reject untrusted
[stderr]   payloads safely.
[stderr] - **Compatibility:** each release declares supported content and save schema
[stderr]   ranges. Incompatible saves are quarantined with their original bytes and a
[stderr]   clear fallback/new-game action; a migration must create a new candidate and
[stderr]   validate it before promotion.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Commands, validation, idempotency, and concurrency
[stderr] 
[stderr] The core exposes typed intent commands such as `StartCampaign`,
[stderr] `ChooseAction`, `UseItem`, `Talk`, `AcceptQuest`, `ClaimReward`,
[stderr] `EnterExit`, `CommitCheckpoint`, `LoadSlot`, and `ApplySettings`. A command
[stderr] contains an expected campaign revision and an interaction/action ID. The core
[stderr] returns either a typed rejection without mutation or an ordered state/event
[stderr] result with a new revision.
[stderr] 
[stderr] - Validate session state, command shape, actor/target existence, availability,
[stderr]   eligibility, quest predicate, and expected revision before mutation. UI
[stderr]   selections are untrusted/stale inputs even though all execution is local.
[stderr] - Use stable `rewardId`/`grantId` ledger entries and interaction IDs. Retrying
[stderr]   an acknowledged reward, quest completion, or checkpoint returns the prior
[stderr]   result rather than granting twice.
[stderr] - One command is processed at a time per active campaign. Disable/debounce
[stderr]   duplicate controls while pending; compare expected revision on completion.
[stderr]   Visibility changes, gamepad repeats, and multiple tabs cannot interleave
[stderr]   transitions. A second tab must be warned/read-only or acquire an explicit
[stderr]   local slot lease; stale writers fail without replacing the latest revision.
[stderr] - Combat resolution uses an explicit deterministic seed/state recorded at
[stderr]   safe boundaries. It never persists an unresolved turn; a crash restores the
[stderr]   prior checkpoint, avoiding half-consumed items, half-applied damage, or
[stderr]   duplicate rewards.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Persistence, migration, and failure semantics
[stderr] 
[stderr] Save commit is a write-ahead replacement protocol: serialize a complete
[stderr] snapshot → validate checksum/schema/invariants → write candidate under a new
[stderr] revision → verify read-back → mark current → retain prior known-good snapshot.
[stderr] Implementations must use browser storage semantics that make the final pointer
[stderr] switch atomic, or emulate it with immutable revision records and a validated
[stderr] current pointer. Never clear the prior save before the new pointer is valid.
[stderr] 
[stderr] | Condition | Required behaviour |
[stderr] | --- | --- |
[stderr] | Storage quota, permission, or unavailable local storage | Keep session playable; show non-blocking save failure and retry guidance; do not claim persistence succeeded. |
[stderr] | Interrupted/crashed write | Ignore incomplete candidate, retain/reload prior confirmed snapshot, and show recovery state at next launch. |
[stderr] | Malformed/checksum-invalid save | Quarantine bytes and metadata; do not overwrite; offer last valid checkpoint/new game and support/export guidance where feasible. |
[stderr] | Migration failure or unsupported version | Preserve original, fail closed into quarantine, and offer clear version/fallback choices. |
[stderr] | Bad content package/reference | Fail build/CI; if discovered at runtime, stop the affected route, retain save, and offer title/retry rather than corrupting campaign state. |
[stderr] | Cache/network refresh failure | Continue with known-good cached release; explain first-install offline limitation without pretending an uncached release is playable. |
[stderr] | Application fault/crash loop | Error boundary offers retry, title, and safe recovery; diagnostics remain local unless explicitly consented and a future transport is approved. |
[stderr] 
[stderr] Migration functions are ordered, pure transformations from one explicit schema
[stderr] version to the next, with fixtures for every supported predecessor. They must
[stderr] be idempotent or record the source version so a retry cannot apply a migration
[stderr] twice. A release may remove old migrations only after a separately approved
[stderr] support/retention decision.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Trust, privacy, and security
[stderr] 
[stderr] - Treat content, imported saves, URL parameters, browser events, service-worker
[stderr]   messages, and persisted bytes as untrusted. Validate and bound sizes before
[stderr]   parsing; never execute content as code or render dialogue via unsafe HTML.
[stderr] - Serve production assets over HTTPS with a restrictive content security policy,
[stderr]   no third-party scripts by default, dependency lock/review, and build-time
[stderr]   asset integrity/hash manifest. Do not embed credentials in the client.
[stderr] - Use same-origin storage isolation; minimise persisted data to game state and
[stderr]   chosen preferences. Do not collect names, contact data, precise location,
[stderr]   advertising IDs, account identifiers, payment data, or gameplay analytics in
[stderr]   P0.
[stderr] - Diagnostics default off where consent is required and must state fields,
[stderr]   purpose, retention, deletion, and transport before collection. A local
[stderr]   diagnostic record excludes free-text dialogue and complete save contents.
[stderr] - There is no authorization role in P0 because there is no server resource or
[stderr]   identity boundary. Locally, a player controls their browser profile; this is
[stderr]   not a security guarantee against a device owner. Any server feature requires
[stderr]   explicit least-privilege roles, authenticated authorization on every request,
[stderr]   rate limits, audit events, deletion/retention policy, incident ownership, and
[stderr]   documented offline/outage behaviour before implementation.
[stderr] - No copied branding, assets, text, layouts, or distinctive interaction
[stderr]   sequences enter the content pipeline. Provenance/review is required for
[stderr]   shipped art, audio, writing, and dependencies.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Target-platform matrix
[stderr] 
[stderr] | Contract area | Responsive public web application | Installable PWA |
[stderr] | --- | --- | --- |
[stderr] | Shared code/data boundary | Same rules core, content package, save schema, semantic actions, accessibility settings, and release compatibility rules. Browser adapter owns viewport/input differences. | Same shared core/data. Service worker, installation, cache lifecycle, and platform install affordance are PWA adapters. |
[stderr] | Input and device capability | Keyboard, mouse, touch, and gamepad where exposed; feature-detect and show available mappings. Handle resize, rotation, focus/visibility, reduced-motion/contrast settings where available. | Same inputs and feature detection; no assumed hardware merely because installed. Installation, notifications, or storage-persistence prompts are optional and must not block play. |
[stderr] | Offline and recovery | May play offline only after relevant assets are cached. First uncached visit tells player an initial download/network is required. Local save protocol applies. | Primary offline route after successful install/cache. Precaches approved shell/content, retains known-good cache on refresh failure, and keeps saves independent of cache eviction. |
[stderr] | Build and test | Produce hashed static assets, manifest, and browser-compatible bundle. Test core/unit/content validation, responsive layouts, input adaptations, save corruption/migration, error states, and supported browser/device/network matrix. | Build includes service worker and install manifest. Test installability, offline reload, cache rollback/update-at-safe-boundary, storage pressure, and all shared checks. |
[stderr] | Accessibility and performance | Keyboard-operable menus, semantic labels, visible focus, readable scalable text, captions, non-colour cues, settings persistence, responsive phone/tablet/desktop checks. Establish approval-gated performance budgets for initial load, interaction frame time, memory, bundle/cache size, and slow-network behaviour before release. | Same accessibility/performance requirements, plus offline messaging, update state, install prompt alternatives, and no inaccessible full-screen/install-only path. Measure cold/warm/offline startup and cache size. |
[stderr] | Release channel and policy | Immutable versioned static release to approved web hosting; staged/canary and rollback process must be selected before launch. Publish supported-browser matrix, privacy notice if diagnostics changes, and recovery notes. | Browser-mediated install/update channel; manifest/icon/display policy and service-worker behaviour must meet supported browser/platform rules. Prompt only at safe boundaries; release notes and rollback retain prior runnable cache. |
[stderr] 
[stderr] Support targets are expressed as capability tests rather than invented minimum
[stderr] OS versions: current and previous major releases of agreed evergreen browsers
[stderr] on representative phone, tablet, and desktop classes; exact versions/devices,
[stderr] browser engine exclusions, storage quota, and slow-network thresholds remain a
[stderr] release approval gate.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Observability, quality evidence, and release verification
[stderr] 
[stderr] P0 observability is local and player-respecting: structured console/build logs
[stderr] in development, release/version/cache status visible in support UI, and an
[stderr] optional bounded local fault record. It has no remote monitoring dependency.
[stderr] Every fault has a player-facing recovery path and a test that asserts retained
[stderr] save/cache behaviour as applicable.
[stderr] 
[stderr] | Evidence layer | Required checks |
[stderr] | --- | --- |
